Every memory access — not just page-table walks — passes through a permission check that happens **in parallel** with translation, using bits stored right in the page table entry (or a companion structure) that the walk already fetched:

```
access request: (virtual address, is_write, current privilege level)
PTE bits checked: Present, Read/Write, User/Supervisor, No-Execute (NX)

if !Present:                          -> page fault (not mapped)
elif is_write and !ReadWrite:          -> page fault (write to read-only page)
elif CPL == 3 and !User:               -> page fault (user code touching kernel page)
elif is_execute and NX_bit_set:         -> page fault (executing non-executable page, e.g. stack/heap)
else:                                   -> access proceeds
```

That NX (no-execute) check is exactly the hardware backing for **DEP/W^X** (write XOR execute) protections modern OSes enable by default — marking every writable page (stack, heap) as non-executable is what turns a classic stack-smashing buffer overflow, which used to jump straight into injected shellcode on the stack, into an instant fault instead.
